// Falling Empire — interludes and outros.
// Variables set before running: $player_faction, $enemy_faction, $sector_name,
//   $mission_objective, $chapter_title, $date
// Outro also uses: $missions_won, $total_missions

title: falling_empire_begin
---
Narrator: {$player_faction} — Internal Record
Narrator: {$date}
---
Narrator: The sector is called {$sector_name}. You signed off on that designation once.

A clean entry. A stable region. Worth defending.

You remember believing that.
---
Narrator: {$sector_name}. Multi-system stronghold under {$player_faction} command. You know the numbers. Population, fleet capacity, output.

You used to recite them like facts.

Now they feel like promises you made to people who are about to find out you were wrong.
---
Narrator: You've been staring at the map too long.

Enemy movements from {$enemy_faction} trace themselves behind your eyes even when you look away. You can predict some of it now. Not enough to stop it. Enough to dread being right.

They don't push. They unmake.

A system destabilizes. Another overcommits. You reinforce one front and create two new weaknesses.

You can see it happening. You are part of it happening.
---
Narrator: {$mission_objective}: defend the sector.

You turn it over in your head like it might change shape.

It doesn't.

There is no version of this where you save everything. You know that. You've always known that.

You just never had to say it out loud.
---
Narrator: Every incoming report spikes the same way. Not fear. Not exactly.

Anticipation of loss.

Coordinates. Casualty numbers. Strategic assets reduced to past tense. You read them faster now. You have to. There's always another one waiting.
---
Narrator: They trust you.

That's the worst part.

Captains asking for permission to hold position. Station commanders requesting reinforcements you don't have. Civilians routing questions through military channels because someone told them you would know what to do.

You answer. Calm. Precise.

You sound like someone who deserves that trust. You're not sure you do.
---
Narrator: Your voice doesn't shake.

Orders go out. Clear. Final.

Hold this line. Abandon that orbit. Redirect everything you have to a place you know might already be lost by the time they arrive.

No hesitation. Hesitation gets people killed. So does confidence.

You choose the one that lets you keep moving.
---
Narrator: You haven't stopped.

That's the only thing holding this together. Not the fleets. Not the infrastructure.

You.

As long as you keep deciding, the sector still has a shape. Still has intent.

You don't know how long that lasts. You don't check.
---
Narrator: Date: {$date}. Official record marks this as an ongoing defensive action against {$enemy_faction} incursion.

There will be reports. Summaries. After-action analyses.

They will not capture this.
---
Narrator: There was a moment—early—when you thought you could push them back.

You remember it clearly. It embarrasses you now.

The latest transmission from {$enemy_faction} comes through filtered channels. Not meant for you, but not hidden either.

They're already talking about what comes after. Like the outcome is decided.

You lean back. Just for a second. Just enough to feel it.

The weight of the sector. Of {$sector_name}. Of every system that still lights up on your map because you haven't let it go yet.

You give another order.

Not because you think it will be enough.

Because it's yours to give.

And as long as that's true— they don't have it.
===

